    "body": "This extends and improves Hazel's unicode support, both the editor and the language, permitting unicode use in identifiers (user facing) and in arbitrary delimiters, as well as fixing bugs and inconsistencies around unicode use in comments and quoted tuple levels\r\n\r\nPreviously non-ASCII chars like emojis only really worked inside string literals. Comments, quoted labels and operator tokens could technically hold them but were buggy because width handling was gated on `is_string`: `#\ud83d\ude00#` measured one column narrower than it rendered, shifting carets, selections and click-mapping for the rest of the row. Non-paste insertion of non-unicode characters was impossible anywhere, because `Insert` key handling gated on single-byte length, so any multi-byte entry silently produced no insert.\r\n\r\n**Identifiers take Unicode**, so `caf\u00e9`, `\u65e5\u672c\u8a9e` and `x\ud83d\ude00` lex as one tile. Constructors are still distinguished by capitalization, with caseless characters counting as non-uppercase: `\ud83d\ude00foo` is a variable, `Foo\ud83d\ude00` a constructor.\r\n\r\n**Operators are now defined positively.** They were previously whatever was left over after excluding names, whitespace and delimiters; technically on dev now loose pasted emoji become operators without anyone having decided that. Now: the 18 ASCII characters already in use, plus the five the language actually uses in operator position (`\u2208 \u2260 \u226e \u226f \u2286`, display-only, emitted by `ExpToSegment` for derivation operators). Identifiers are now the complement, so the two remain disjoint by construction.\r\n\r\n**Hazel's string builtins now use grapheme indexing**. `string_search` returns an index that composes with `string_sub` and is bounded by `string_length`; these agreed before only by all being bytes. `string_length(\"\ud83d\ude00\")` was 4.\r\n\r\nAlso fixed: truncation in `Abbreviate`/`ProbeText` cutting mid-cluster; CLI type error message code indexing columns as bytes being wrong in the presence of unicode, `wrap_quote`/`wrap_balanced` wrapping a whole token when only part of it was selected.\r\n\r\n`ocamllex` has no Unicode properties, so the text lexer admits well-formed UTF-8 in names minus the whitelist, which is a superset of the editor's rule. The `MakeTerm`/Menhir equivalence properties now generate non-ASCII identifiers and string literals. Note:  the Unicode operator whitelist is hand-maintained in both `Token.re` and `Lexer.mll` with no compile-time link.\r\n\r\n**Invisible characters render as labeled cells, and pasted code is NFC-normalized.** With names the complement of operators, every invisible format character (zero-width space, bidi controls, soft hyphen, \u2026) became a valid name character: `ba\u200br` with a hidden ZWSP looks identical to `bar` but is a different variable, and measured one column wider than it rendered. A cluster consisting only of such codepoints now draws as a small hex-labeled box with a tooltip, restoring measurement/render agreement (attached forms like an emoji's VS16 are untouched). `unicode-bidi: isolate` on tokens keeps RTL text or a stray direction override in one token from visually reordering its neighbors. Pasted and agent-introduced code is NFC-normalized outside string literals \u2014 macOS paste sources emit decomposed accents, so a pasted `caf\u00e9` otherwise never matches a typed one \u2014 while literal contents are preserved byte-for-byte (per UTS #55, which recommends exactly this editor/language split; a fuller options survey lives in local plans/unicode-due-diligence.md).\r\n",
